ys curious like all marvel fans  about easter eggs are there any specific  scenes  in the first two episodes that you would  advise  fans to hit pause on  oh just trying to honestly i would say  like  these are more just fun details but the  drink that mobius is drinking in the  time theatre scene  is like a drink that's not around  anymore and we've got a lot of fun stuff  like that across the tva  like stuff that doesn't necessarily  exist within  you know our time now on earth like but  you know the tva you can get that kind  of stuff which i think is fun  um i also have a few references to  marvel across it like there's a shop  that's an homage to iron man which i  think  any fan of iron man will probably spot  quite quickly  um so yeah those are the kind of things  but i wouldn't want to lead  the witness too much because i just  think it's fun to kind of spot that  stuff right so  yeah completely uh what was it like  actually the thing that i think one of  the things that i love about the series  the most is that you're exploring a new  part of the mcu something that's never  come before  and so what is it like as a director  establishing the rules and regulations  if you will  of this new organization and how much  freedom do you have versus  someone overseeing you at marvel saying  oh we got to be careful with this  honestly i think i've never felt like  with marvel it was always whatever's  best for story then we'll work it out  and i think  the really fun thing for us as a team in  creating the tva  was just i mean for me like i've worked  in a lot of offices  it took me a long time to get my first  directing gig and  i think something for me was like well i  want this to feel like a real living  breathing office so these guys like  never stop working there should be a  coffee pot that looks like it hasn't  been changed for  far too long and there should be posters  telling you to keep your desk tidy  and just the messiness and the realness  of the place like i was really keen to  explore that  and alongside that i think the fun of it  was like i was really  inspired by like brutalist architecture  because where i grew up in southeast  london like i grew up near where they  filmed a clockwork orange  and children and men and i just i love  the use of that and obviously blade  runner uses a lot of architecture like  that and i was excited by seeing that in  the mcu but mixing that with this kind  of midwest  you know very classy style because you  know the tv are very heroic and  the idea of melding those together was  really exciting to me  along with like um like i guess like the  retro futuristic technology like i was  really excited  in like working with russell bobber on  who's that who is our prop  like working on like the weapon design  and how the computers would look with  kazra my production designer and  i think something that was exciting to  me was just kind of pulling from all  these different areas of sci-fi like i  kind of wanted the show to almost be  like a big love letter to sci-fi like um  our time doors are inspired by dune for  example but we also have like  um the font on the computers was  inspired by alien  and so we were kind of you know we were  pulling from everywhere but i think with  all those sci-fi references  and the overall style reference it was  also just always about giving it a  groundedness and always the emotion is  what would ground us in the  you know the circumstances we were in so  like  for example as you see when loki's first  brought in by the tva  we really wanted that i mean for me i  wanted that to represent what it was  like going through the airport  you know and like having to go for all  the security changes and like all the  weird queues you have to go in or i  guess in america the dmv  so i think it was always trying to bring  like  bits of reality that the audience would  recognize um but putting that into a  fantastical setting  i have to stop there i just want